=== BTK

Before we move on, #link("https://www.forbes.com/sites/wadeshepard/2016/12/15/reconnecting-asia-the-story-behind-the-emerging-baku-tbilisi-kars-rail-line/")[we should probably discuss the importance of the] @BTK:a
@forbes-btk
given its high praise from Bin Sulayem.

The @BTK:a served as a critical geopolitical and economic linchpin in Eurasia,
#index[BTK]
capturing global attention as a primary conduit of the emerging Middle Corridor and China's One Belt One Road initiative.
#index[China]

Strategically, the rail corridor was vital because it physically bypassed both
Armenia
#index[Armenia]
(due to regional territorial conflicts)
and Russian #index[Russia]
territory giving Central Asian nations and Azerbaijan direct unhindered access to European markets through Turkey
#index[Turkey].
@forbes-btk

By solidifying the economic and political alignment between
Azerbaijan #index[Azerbaijan] (Baku),
Georgia #index[Georgia] (Tbilisi), and Turkey (Ankara), the project underscored a major shift toward regional self-reliance
and transformed the area into an indispensable transport hub for international trade and altered the balance of power
across the post-Soviet region.
@forbes-btk

For the Kremlin, the realization of the @BTK:a railway represents a profound challenge to Moscow’s historic economic hegemony and
transport monopoly over the former Soviet republics.
By offering an alternative that circumvents Russian territory entirely, the rail line erodes the strategic leverage of the
Trans-Siberian routes and diminishes the Kremlin's ability to dictate trade conditions to Central Asia and the South Caucasus.
@forbes-btk

For Beijing
#index[China],
the corridor serves as a vital, rapidly ascending link in China’s One Belt One Road initiative, providing an elite,
high-speed maritime bypass to transport Chinese goods directly into the heart of Europe via the Middle Corridor.
By diversifying transit options away from volatile global maritime routes, the rail link solidifies China’s long-term commercial
reach across Central Asia.
@forbes-btk

For Washington
#index[US], the @BTK:a is viewed as a critical proving ground for Western-backed efforts to break the Russo-Iranian monopoly on
Eurasian energy and supply line corridors, effectively anchoring Caspian nations deeper into Western economic orbits.
By backing projects that strengthen ties between Azerbaijan, Georgia, and Turkey, the @US:a directly advances its strategic priority
of reinforcing regional autonomy away from traditional adversarial superpowers.
@forbes-btk

This intense infrastructural friction directly mirrors the geopolitical drama that played out on the grandmaster boards during the
2015 FIDE World Cup #index[FIDE] in Baku #index[Baku].
Just as the tournament served as an aggressive soft-power arena where national sporting dominance intersected with volatile
international rivalries, the @BTK:a railway stands as a hard-power manifestation of the exact same regional power struggles.
Operating along the same highly charged geographic fault lines, the competition to control and profit from Eurasian transport
corridors echoes the precise strategic weight of the Baku games, proving that whether through "Brain Power" diplomacy
or steel rails, the South Caucasus remains a high-stakes flashpoint for global superpower rivalries.
@forbes-btk
